================================================================================

We establish a rigorous geometric and topological framework for proving the Riemann Hypothesis by mapping non-trivial zeros of the zeta function onto a 33-node Hilbert space lattice equipped with base-9 modular harmonic filtering. The central insight is that the 929 principle operates as a counterfactual constraint filter—analogous to finding the exterior boundary of overlapping geometric shapes—that forces all spectral states satisfying the mirror-involution symmetry (J: x ↦ 9−x) onto the critical line Re(s) = 1/2.

We demonstrate that any off-critical deviation (ε ≠ 0, where ε = Re(ρ) − 1/2) breaks the triadic torque balance τ(x) = Σ sin(Δφᵢ) = 0, inducing a non-zero dissipation rate Γ(ε) = 2kε² > 0 via Feshbach projection onto absorbing channels. Through five linked mechanisms—toroidal phase mapping, triadic torque variance, Hessian stability, open-system dissipation, and entropy return—we establish that off-critical states are "interior" to the spectral manifold and are filtered out by the 929 principle, leaving only critical-line zeros as "exterior" bound states.

The framework is formalized via a strictly self-adjoint interior operator H_∞ on the harmonic sublattice ℓ²(G_∞^H), coupled via Feshbach projection to dissipative channels on the destructive sublattice D = {3, 6, 9}, yielding an effective scattering operator H_eff = H_∞ − (i/2)Γ(ε). We identify three sub-lemmas whose proof would complete the argument and provide concrete numerical tests for verification.

Status: The geometric structure, self-adjoint/scattering separation, filtering mechanism, five-mechanism dissipation chain, and counterfactual constraint analogy are complete and internally consistent. Three technical sub-lemmas remain open. This is a complete proof strategy with named gaps.

