SECTION 1: INTRODUCTION & CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS
================================================================================

1.1 The Riemann Hypothesis and Its Significance

The Riemann Hypothesis (RH) asserts that every non-trivial zero ρ of the Riemann zeta function ζ(s) satisfies Re(ρ) = 1/2. Formulated in 1859, it remains one of mathematics' deepest unsolved problems.

The zeta function is defined for Re(s) > 1 by:
ζ(s) = Σ(n=1 to ∞) 1/n^s

and extends meromorphically to the complex plane with a simple pole at s = 1. The completed zeta function,
ξ(s) = (1/2)s(s-1)π^(-s/2)Γ(s/2)ζ(s)

satisfies the functional equation ξ(s) = ξ(1−s), which implies that non-trivial zeros come in conjugate pairs reflected across Re(s) = 1/2. The critical line Re(s) = 1/2 is the fixed axis of this reflection symmetry.

1.2 The Hilbert-Pólya Program and Spectral Approaches

The Hilbert-Pólya Conjecture proposes that the non-trivial zeros of ζ(s) are the eigenvalues of a self-adjoint operator. This reframes RH as a spectral problem: self-adjointness guarantees a real spectrum, placing all eigenvalues on the critical line by construction.

However, standard spectral approaches have failed to isolate discrete zero ordinates from continuous spectra. The missing ingredient is a mechanism—a topological or dynamical principle that explains why the critical line is the only admissible locus for stationary bound states.

1.3 The Geometric Filtering Approach

This work proposes that the answer lies in counterfactual constraint filtering: a discrete, modular structure that admits only certain positions as unitary channels and treats forbidden positions as absorbing dissipative sinks. Within such a structure, off-critical states decay rather than persist as stationary eigenstates.

The analogy is geometric: just as the exterior boundary of overlapping rectangles is uniquely determined by filtering out interior points, the critical line is uniquely determined by filtering out off-critical deviations via the 929 principle.

1.4 The 929 Principle as Counterfactual Constraint

Definition 1.1 (The 929 Principle):

The 929 principle is a modular filtering constraint that organizes stable configurations around a central anchor point. It operates in three layers:

1. Central Anchor (9): A fixed core V₀ with Dirichlet condition ψ(V₀) = 0
2. Intermediate Coupling (2): A phase-locking layer that couples neighbors via triadic meetings
3. Outer Boundary (9): An exterior shell structure that determines which states are "interior" (absorbed) vs "exterior" (stable)

Under this constraint, positions are classified by their mod-9 digital root:

- Harmonic channels: H = {1, 2, 4, 5, 7, 8} = (ℤ/9ℤ)ˣ (unitary, stable, allowed)
- Destructive zones: D = {3, 6, 9} = 3ℤ/9ℤ (non-unitary sinks, unstable, forbidden)

Central Hypothesis: The Riemann zeros, when viewed via a spectral transfer map Φ into a lattice system built on G_∞, are forced onto the critical line because any off-critical deviation couples into the destructive zones, triggering exponential scattering dissipation rather than unitary stationarity. Off-critical states are "interior" to the spectral manifold and are filtered out; only critical-line zeros remain as "exterior" bound states.

